2. BSP Kernel and Distros
=========================

The following table summarizes the valid Wind River Linux distros for this BSP.
'Y' in each content cell stands for supported; 'N' stands for not supported:

  +--------------+-------------+------------------+
  | valid/distro |   wrlinux   | wrlinux-std-sato |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+
  |    valid     |      Y      |         Y        |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+

For the supported kernel type for this BSP, check the TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES
by running 'bitbake -e | grep "^TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES"'.

Note: The preempt-rt kernel type can be used with this BSP/Machine.

3. Board Specific Patches
=========================

To get a list of patches applied to the kernel specific to this BSP along with
patch descriptions use git-whatchanged on the default kernel. For example:

	%> cd tmp-glibc/work-shared/<bsp_name>/kernel-source
	%> git whatchanged <kernel_version>/standard/base..<kernel_version>/standard/<bsp_name>


4. Boot Instructions
====================

4.1 Device Trees
----------------
Now the ZCU102 board is required to use a device tree to boot Linux.
Two types of device tree files are provided and different files are
suitable for different scenarios.

4.1.1 Kernel Device Trees
-------------------------
The kernel device tree files describe the Versal board hardware features,
except for FPGA implementations. In other words, these files are only used
in scenarios without FPGA implementations.

The device tree files and their target boards are listed below:

	zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0.dtb

They will be installed to <PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/
and the first partition of the WIC image along with the kernel Image.

The device tree source files are located at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work-shared/amd-zynqmp/kernel-source/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx

They can be modified and compiled by executing the following commands:
	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ bitbake -c devshell virtual/kernel
	(build-host)$ vim arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx/the_file_you_edit*.dts
	(build-host)$ make xilinx/zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0.dtb

Then the new dtb files can be found at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work/amd_zynqmp-wrs-linux/linux-yocto/<kernel_version>/linux-amd_zynqmp-standard-build/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx/

4.1.2 system.dtb
----------------
Different from the kernel device trees above, system.dtb is used for scenarios
with FPGA. It is generated by the device-tree recipe in meta-xilinx. Its
principle is to generate system.dtb based on board dts files downloaded from
https://github.com/Xilinx/device-tree-xlnx.git and Programming Logic dtsi file
pl.dtsi which is extracted from the Xilinx Support Archive(xsa) file.

If "DISTRO_FEATURES:append = ' xilinx-bootloader'" is added to conf/local.conf,
the device-tree recipe will generate system.dtb, install it to
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/
and the first partition of the WIC image.

The system.dtb should be used as long as the system involves FPGA implementation.

4.2 Boot Mode
-------------
The ZCU102 boards supports three boot modes: SD card, QSPI32 and JTAG..

4.2.1 SD Boot Mode
------------------
First refer to the section "7.1 BOOT.BIN" to build a bootable WIC
image and burn it to SD card at host-pc:

   (build-host)$ sudo dd if=wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.wic of=/dev/sdaX

Plug the SD card into the ZCU102 board SD socket and set the DIP
switch SW6 setting like:

   SW6[4]  SW6[3]  SW6[2]  SW6[1]
   OFF     OFF     OFF     ON

Power on the ZCU102 board or press the POR button SW2.

4.2.2 QSPI32 Boot Mode
----------------------
First we boot the board with SD Card by following the steps in the
section "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode". At the U-Boot stage, when the message
"Hit any key to stop autoboot:" appears, hit any key, then run the
following commands to flash the images on the QSPI daughter card:

   (u-boot)> sf probe 0 0 0
   (u-boot)> sf erase 0x0 0x4000000
   (u-boot)> fatls mmc 0:1
   (u-boot)> fatload mmc 0:1 0x70000000 BOOT.bin
   (u-boot)> sf write 0x70000000 0x0 <BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>

Note that the "<BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>" is a hexadecimal value of
the size of BOOT.BIN.

After flashing the images, turn off the power switch on the board,
and change the SW6 boot mode pin settings to QSPI boot mode:

   SW6[4]  SW6[3]  SW6[2]  SW6[1]
   ON      ON      OFF     ON

Power cycle the board. The board now boots up using the BOOT.BIN in
the QSPI flash.

4.2.3 JTAG Boot Mode
--------------------
The JTAG Boot Mode mainly uses xsct on host-pc and it is out of the scope
of this README. Please refer to the document Zynq UltraScale+ Device Technical
Reference Manual(UG1085).

4.3 Boot Linux From U-Boot
--------------------------
This section provides the examples about how to setup u-boot to boot
Linux via network and SD Card.

4.3.1 Network
-------------
First you will need a host machine with the network servers TFTP and NFS
correctly installed and configured.

First find the compressed archive rootfs file at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.tar.bz2

Then extract this file to <nfsroot_path>:
   (build-host)$ sudo tar jxf wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.tar.bz2 -C <nfsroot_path>

And copy the kernel and dtb file to <tftpserver_path>:
   (build-host)$ cp Image <tftpserver_path>
   (build-host)$ cp system.dtb <tftpserver_path>

Set the u-boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv serverip <tftpserver_ip>; setenv ipaddr <board_ip>
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs console=ttyPS0,115200 earlycon=cdns,mmio,0xFF000000 clk_ignore_unused root=/dev/nfs rw nfsroot=$serverip:<nfsroot_path> ip=dhcp
   (u-boot)> tftpboot 0x10000000 Image; tftpboot 0x18800000 system.dtb; booti 0x10000000 - 0x18800000

4.3.2 SD Card
-------------
If DISTRO_FEATURES contains "xilinx-bootloader", there is a boot.scr that
contains U-Boot commands to boot Linux from SD Card automatically. There
is no need to reconfigure u-boot.

If DISTRO_FEATURES does not contain "xilinx-bootloader", execute the following
commands to set the u-boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv bootfile Image; setenv fdtfile zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0.dtb; setenv loadaddr 0x10000000; setenv fdtaddr 0x18800000;
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs 'onsole=ttyPS0,115200 root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 rw rootwait earlycon=cdns,mmio,0xFF000000 clk_ignore_unused'
   (u-boot)> setenv bootcmd 'fatload mmc 0:1 $loadaddr $bootfile; fatload mmc 0:1 $fdtaddr $fdtfile; booti $loadaddr - $fdtaddr';
   (u-boot)> saveenv; run bootcmd;

5 WIC Notes
===========

User can use the OpenEmbedded Image Creator to create the properly partitioned
image on a SD card. It generates partitioned images from existing OpenEmbedded
build artifacts. Please refer to the following URL for more detailed partition
information about WIC:

https://docs.yoctoproject.org/singleindex.html#creating-partitioned-images-using-wic

This BSP supports disk images for SD card.
After building the project, users will get a WIC image under the directory
tmp/deploy/images/<bsp name>/, such as:

	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-zynqmp/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.wic

NOTE: The WIC image built in WRLinux does not contain the bootloader BOOT.BIN
by default. Please refer to the section 7.1 "BOOT.BIN" for the detailed
description about how to create a bootable WIC image.

To burn WIC image to SD card, users only needs to execute the below command:

	(build-host)$ sudo dd if=wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-zynqmp.rootfs.wic of=/dev/your_sd_dev

6 Common Features
=================

6.1 Multilib
------------
By default, this BSP is configured as a 64-bit kernel and 64-bit userspace.
If a full 32-bit filesystem is necessary, use the prefix "lib32-" before
the expected target image as follows:

	# bitbake lib32-wrlinux-image-std

6.2 kexec and kdump
-------------------

You need to add feature/kexec support as follow:
	./wrlinux-x/setup.sh --machines amd-zynqmp --templates feature/kexec,feature/kdump

6.2.1 kexec
-----------

	kexec -l  /boot/Image --append="`cat /proc/cmdline`"
	kexec -e

6.2.2 kdump
-----------

Add crashkernel=512M to the kernel cmdline
   kexec -p /boot/Image --append="$your-bootcmd"
   echo c > /proc/sysrq-trigger

6.3 CAN
-------

# configure can0 in the speed of 800000
   $ ip link set can0 up type can bitrate 800000
   $ ip -details link show can0

# send out a single frame
   $ cansend can0 1F334455#1122334455667788
   $ ip link set can0 down

6.4 DisplayPort
---------------

According to the vendor, only some monitors in the following link are supported:
  https://www.xilinx.com/support/answers/68671.html

6.4.1 DP Display
----------------

Show and adjust screen resolution, some draw examples
   root@amd-zynqmp:~# export DISPLAY=:0.0
   root@amd-zynqmp:~# xrandr
   Screen 0: minimum 320 x 200, current 1024 x 768, maximum 4096 x 4096
   DP-1 connected primary 1024x768+0+0 518mm x 324mm
      1920x1080     24.00    23.98
      1280x720      60.00    50.00    59.94
      1024x768      75.03*   60.00
      800x600       75.00    60.32
      720x576       50.00
      720x480       60.00    59.94
      640x480       75.00    60.00    59.94
      720x400       70.08
   root@amd-zynqmp:~# xrandr -s 1920x1080

6.4.2 DP Audio
--------------

Connect loudspeaker or headset with display monitor, and then run below commands:
	# aplay -l
	# speaker-test -c2 -twav

6.5 suspend/resume
------------------

"Suspend-To-RAM" is supportted by running below command:

	# echo mem > /sys/power/state

Wakeup system, customer can use network, rtc, console and so on, it depends on concrete scenario.
Below is an example of wakeing up by console.
Run below command before entering suspend status.
	# echo enabled > /sys/devices/platform/amba/ff000000.serial/tty/ttyPS0/power/wakeup
And then, press Enter key in console window.

6.6 System Monitor
------------------

System monitor includes PL monitor and PS monitor.
Each system monitor measures voltage and temperature to provide information and alarms
to other parts of the system including the PMU, RPU, and APU processors.

Customer could check the detail system monitor interfaces with below command:
	# ls -la /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:device0

6.7 CANFD
---------

Based on CANFD FPGA LogiCORE IP from Xilinx, there are 2 canfd devices on FPGA side.
An example is provided to verify can/canfd driver with Xilinx-zcu102 board(REV 1.0) and
FMC extend board(https://www.xilinx.com/products/boards-and-kits/1-12nths4.html)

6.7.1 Boot up system with CANFD specific dtb file
------------------------------------------------

	# fatload mmc 0 0x13000000 zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0-canfd.dtb
	or
	# tftpboot 0x13000000 <tftp server>/zynqmp-zcu102-rev1.0-canfd.dtb

6.7.2 Verify CANFD
------------------

	Detail verification commands as below:

	# ip link set can1 up type can bitrate 125000 dbitrate 8000000 fd on
	# ip link set can2 up type can bitrate 125000 dbitrate 8000000 fd on
	# candump can1 &
	# cansend can2 5A1#11.22.33.44.55.66.77.88
	# ip -d -s link show can1
	# ip -d -s link show can2

If can1 dump data from can2, CANFD feature works fine.

There is a Xilinx Wiki link as below, it is a CANFD development guide.
It may offer some help when customer develops CANFD feature on their own platform.
https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/18842496/Linux+CAN+driver

If customer wants to implement their own or more canfd devices, please connect with
Xilinx to get professional technical support about how to design CANFD on FPGA side.

6.8 USB
-------

The USB interface on the PS-side serves multiple roles as a host, device, and OTG
controller. Please change kernel options and dts file according to the descriptions
in the following link:

  https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/18841729/Zynq+Ultrascale+MPSOC+Linux+USB+device+driver

And also, the USB Jumper Settings on the board ZCU102 are described below.

6.8.1 USB Host
--------------

    J109    J110    J112    J113    J7
    2-3     2-3     1-2     1-2     ON

6.8.2 USB Device
----------------

    J109    J110    J112    J113    J7
    2-3     1-2     1-2     1-2     OFF

In addition to the method which modifies "dr_mode" property in dts file, you can change this property
by using "fdt" u-boot command. The example for USB device mode is as described below:

	=> tftpboot 0x10000000 Image; tftpboot 0x13800000 dtb
	=> fdt addr 0x13800000; fdt resize; fdt set /axi/usb0@ff9d0000/dwc3@fe200000 dr_mode peripheral
	=> booti 0x10000000 - 0x13800000

6.8.3 USB OTG
-------------

    J109    J110    J112    J113    J7
    1-2     1-2     1-2     2-3     ON

The example to change "dr_mode" to "otg" is as described below:

	=> tftpboot 0x10000000 Image; tftpboot 0x13800000 dtb
	=> fdt addr 0x13800000; fdt resize; fdt set /axi/usb0@ff9d0000/dwc3@fe200000 dr_mode otg
	=> booti 0x10000000 - 0x13800000

NOTE: USB OTG mode can only work at USB2.0 speed for host & device, and file this question to
the vendor, the following link is their answer:
  https://support.xilinx.com/s/question/0D52E00006hpgaGSAQ/otg-in-zcu102-with-rev-1-0-doesnt-work-on-the-latest-sdk-v20174

6.9 PCIe
--------

Only some limited PCIe devices were supported based on the SDK2018.02 and the
following link:

  http://www.wiki.xilinx.com/ZynqMP+Linux+PCIe+Root+Port

As mentioned in this website, NVMe SSD and TIGON3 have been validated. It is not
recommended to use other PCIe devices(Not mentioned in this website) because it
might bring the board with a little electrical instability on ZCU102 Rev 1.0.

6.10 GPU
--------

3D-graphics apps call Mali-libs API(libmali-xlnx) to drive the Mali400 GPU
directly by Mali400 kernel driver.
Some of mesa-demos test cases can be used to validate this function under limitation,
but we just keep this part as-is without further debugging since only binary
libs are available.

Due to the software license terms, we are not permitted to redistribute the
libmali-xlnx components. Due to this, we are unable to support the components
or directly enable them within Wind River Linux. Please contact your silicon
vendor if you have any questions about the libmali-xlnx software license terms.

As below is the step how to build a graphics enabled system:

  1) set BB_NO_NETWORK as '0' in <path-of-project>/build/conf/local.conf file.

  2) Launch bitbake to build an image with graphics support

     $ cd path_to_your_project
     $ . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux
     $ . ./oe-init-build-env
     $ bitbake-layers add-layer <path-of-layers>/meta-xilinx/meta-xilinx-mali400
     $ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
DISTRO ?= "wrlinux-graphics"
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_virtual/mesa:amd-zynqmp = "mesa-gl"
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_virtual/libgl:amd-zynqmp = "mesa-gl"
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_virtual/libgles1:amd-zynqmp = "libmali-xlnx"
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_virtual/libgles2:amd-zynqmp = "libmali-xlnx"
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_virtual/egl:amd-zynqmp = "libmali-xlnx"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += "libmali-xlnx"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'kernel-module-mali'
LICENSE_FLAGS_ACCEPTED += "xilinx"
MACHINE_FEATURES:append = " mali400"
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " libmali"
_EOF
     $ bitbake wrlinux-image-std-sato

6.11 SATA
---------

When your SATA hard disk data is corrupted, the system might report a hard link
reset, then try the following command:

     $ echo "- - -" > /sys/class/scsi_host/host1/scan
       ata2: hard resetting link
       ata2: SATA link down (SStatus 0 SControl 330)
       ata2: EH complete

And mkfs a new filesystem on your hard disk partition.

7 Xilinx Specific Features
==========================

7.1 BOOT.BIN
------------
BOOT.BIN is the primary boot image file for Zynq UltraScale+ MPSoC devices.
It contains all necessary components to boot the system, including the First
Stage Boot Loader (FSBL), PMU firmware, and application images. The file is
generated using the Bootgen tool and supports both secure and non-secure boot
configurations.

BOOT.BIN Detailed Structure:
	BOOT.BIN
	├── Boot Header
	├── FSBL
	├── PMU Firmware (optional)
	├── Bitstream (optional)
	├── ATF (ARM Trusted Firmware)
	└── Application Images
	    └── U-Boot (Secondary bootloader)

There are three ways to generate BOOT.BIN:
	- Using prebuilt firmwares
	- Using meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp layer
	- Using SDT workflow

7.1.1 Prebuilt Firmwares
------------------------
If you just need a bootable BOOT.BIN to boot Linux, but do not want to compile
it, you can use the prebuilt firmwares provided by AMD to generate one directly.
The following example shows the steps for generating BOOT.BIN for ZCU102.

	(build-host)$ git clone https://github.com/Xilinx/soc-prebuilt-firmware.git
	(build-host)$ cd soc-prebuilt-firmware
	(build-host)$ git checkout xlnx_rel_v2025.2
	(build-host)$ cd zcu102-zynqmp
	(build-host)$ bootgen -arch zynqmp -image bootgen.bif -o ./BOOT.BIN -w

The bootgen tool used above is in <path_to_Vivado>/bin/bootgen. If you do not
have Vivado/Vitis installed, you can download the source code of bootgen, compile
it, and then use it after installation.

	(build-host)$ git clone https://github.com/Xilinx/bootgen.git
	(build-host)$ cd bootgen; git checkout xlnx_rel_v2025.2
	(build-host)$ make; make install

The generated BOOT.BIN can be directly copied to the first FAT32 partition
of the SD card or to be installed to the WIC image by executing the following
steps:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
IMAGE_BOOT_FILES:append = " /< path_to_BOOT.BIN >/BOOT.BIN;BOOT.BIN"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

7.1.2 meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp layer
--------------------------------------
WRLinux uses the machine configuration files in meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp
and the recipes from meta-xilinx to automate the entire process by building
all required components, generating the appropriate BIF file and calling
bootgen to create the final BOOT.BIN image.

The following steps are to generate BOOT.BIN using the configuration files
in meta-amd-adaptive-socs-bsp.

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
    (build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fsbl'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'arm-trusted-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'pmufw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bitstream'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'pmu-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'fsbl-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-uenv'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'trusted-firmware-a'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " xilinx-bootloader"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:meta-python += 'python3-humanfriendly'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:virtualization-layer += 'lopper'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'python3-anytree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'binutils'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone-sdt += 'sdt-artifacts aiefal aienginev2 audio-formatter avbuf axicdma axicdma-example axidma axidma-example axiethernet axiethernet-example axipcie axipmon axipmon-example axis-switch axivdma axivdma-example bram bram-example can can-example canfd canfd-example canps canps-example cframe cfupmc clk-wiz clk-wiz-example clockps clockps-example common coresightps-dcc csi csi2tx csi2txss csudma csudma-example dcsub ddrcpsu devcfg devcfg-example dfeccf dfeccf-example dfeequ dfeequ-example dfemix dfemix-example dfeofdm dfeofdm-example dfeprach dfeprach-example dfxasm dfxasm-example dmaps dmaps-example dp12 dp12rxss dp12txss dp14 dp14rxss dp14txss dp21 dp21rxss dp21txss dpdma dphy dppsu dsi dsi2rx dsirxss dsitxss dual-splitter emaclite emaclite-example emacps emacps-example emc gpio gpio-example gpiops gpiops-example hdcp1x hdcp22-cipher hdcp22-cipher-dp hdcp22-common hdcp22-mmult hdcp22-rng hdcp22-rx hdcp22-rx-dp hdcp22-tx hdcp22-tx-dp hwicap i2srx i2stx i3c i3cpsx i3cpsx-example iic iic-example iicps iicps-example intc intc-example iomodule ipipsu ipipsu-example llfifo llfifo-example mbox mboxpsu-exampmcdma mcdma-example mipicsiss mipi-rx-phy mipi-tx-phy mmidp mutex mutex-example nandps nandpsu nandpsu-example ospipsv ospipsv-example pciepsu pciepsu-example prc prd qspips qspips-example qspipsu qspipsu-example resetps resetps-example rfdc rfdc-example rtcpsu rtcpsu-example scugic scugic-example scutimer scuwdt sd-fec sdiaud sdi-common sdps sdps-example spdif spi spips spips-example sysmon sysmon-example sysmonpsu sysmonpsu-example sysmonpsv sysmonpsv-example tft tmrctr tmrctr-example tmr-inject tmr-manager trafgen trafgen-example trngpsv trngpsx ttcps ttcps-example uartlite uartlite-example uartns550 uartns550-example uartps uartps-example uartpsv uartpsv-example ufspsxc usb usb-example usbps usbpsu usbpsu-example v-axi4s-remap v-csc v-deinterlacer v-demosaic v-demosaict v-gamma-lfrmbuf-rd v-frmbuf-rd-example v-frmbuf-wr v-frmbuf-wr-exa